## Goal
Create a principal component analysis (PCA) of the RNA-seq samples from the gene counts, with each sample coloured by its condition, to visualize the leading patterns of variation across samples.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. In a Python script run in the `gars-bio` environment via the batch executor, load the resolved gene-count matrix and design table with pandas; use `gene_id` as the gene index and the six sample columns matched by `sample_id`.
2. Validate that the count matrix has all six design sample IDs and nonnegative numeric values. Exclude genes with counts below 10 in all six samples.
3. For each retained gene, calculate counts per million using each sample’s total gene count as its library size, then transform values as `log2(CPM + 1)`. Center each gene across samples, without scaling gene variances, and run PCA with scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=2)` on the transposed matrix (samples as observations).
4. Save the first two component scores with sample ID and condition, and create a scatter plot of PC1 versus PC2 coloured by condition, with sample IDs labelled and axis labels showing the percentage variance explained. Save all outputs under `results/`; inputs remain read-only.

